"use strict";(self.webpackChunkkahunakv_docs=self.webpackChunkkahunakv_docs||[]).push([[8749],{1895:e=>{e.exports=JSON.parse('{"archive":{"blogPosts":[{"id":"reusable-transaction-coordinator","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/reusable-transaction-coordinator","editUrl":"https://github.com/kahunakv.github.io/tree/tree/main/blog/2026-07-11-reusable-transaction-coordinator/index.md","source":"@site/blog/2026-07-11-reusable-transaction-coordinator/index.md","title":"Making Distributed Transactions Easier to Trust","description":"Distributed transactions are difficult for a simple reason: a transaction is more than its final commit. It is a conversation made of reads, writes, locks, retries, timeouts, and sometimes failures at the worst possible moment. If each client has to remember every detail of that conversation, correctness depends too much on code outside the database.","date":"2026-07-11T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"kahuna","permalink":"/blog/tags/kahuna"},{"inline":true,"label":"transactions","permalink":"/blog/tags/transactions"},{"inline":true,"label":"distributed-systems","permalink":"/blog/tags/distributed-systems"}],"readingTime":8.68,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Andres Gutierrez","title":"Creator of Kahuna","url":"https://github.com/andresgutierrez","imageURL":"https://github.com/andresgutierrez.png","key":"andresgutierrez","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"reusable-transaction-coordinator","title":"Making Distributed Transactions Easier to Trust","authors":["andresgutierrez"],"tags":["kahuna","transactions","distributed-systems"]},"unlisted":false,"nextItem":{"title":"Why I Built Kahuna - A New Take on Distributed Key/Value Storage","permalink":"/blog/welcome"}},"content":"Distributed transactions are difficult for a simple reason: a transaction is more than its final commit. It is a conversation made of reads, writes, locks, retries, timeouts, and sometimes failures at the worst possible moment. If each client has to remember every detail of that conversation, correctness depends too much on code outside the database.\\n\\nWe want Kahuna to take more responsibility for this process. The direction is to build a reusable transaction coordinator that owns the transaction state on the server, provides one consistent protocol to clients, and makes failure behavior easier to understand.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## One Handle, One Source of Truth\\n\\nToday, a client can participate in coordination by tracking modified keys, acquired locks, and read dependencies. This works, but it creates an uncomfortable question: what happens if the client forgets one item when it asks to commit?\\n\\nThe server should not have to trust a list assembled at the last moment. It already sees the operations, so it should build the transaction\'s working set as those operations succeed.\\n\\nWith the new model, the client will keep an opaque transaction handle. This handle will identify the transaction and route requests back to its coordinator. Reads, writes, lock operations, commit, and rollback will all use it. The details of the working set will remain on the server.\\n\\nThis gives us a clear source of truth. When a key is modified, the coordinator records it. When a lock is acquired or released, the coordinator updates its state. When a read must be checked for a conflict, the coordinator keeps the observation. Commit and rollback will use this server-owned state instead of accepting coordination lists from the caller.\\n\\nFor application developers, the public API should feel smaller. For the system, the rules become stronger because a caller cannot accidentally omit part of the transaction.\\n\\n## Closing the Door Before Finalization\\n\\nThere is a subtle race in any interactive transaction. Imagine that one request is writing a key while another request starts commit. If commit takes a snapshot of the working set too early, the write may finish without being included. If it takes the snapshot too late, finalization may wait forever for new operations.\\n\\nWe plan to solve this with a clear closing step. The first close, commit, or rollback request will stop the transaction from accepting new operations. It will then wait for operations that were already registered to finish. Only after that will the coordinator freeze an immutable view of the working set.\\n\\nThis frozen view will be reused by every later finalization attempt. It also gives integrations a safe moment to do pre-commit work. For example, a database using Kahuna may need the final list of modified keys before publishing cache changes. A normal status query is useful while the transaction is open, but only the closed snapshot can promise that no late write will appear.\\n\\nThe important idea is simple: finalization starts by closing the door, then waits for everyone who was already inside.\\n\\n## Safe Retries for Every Operation\\n\\nNetworks make outcomes uncertain. A server may complete a write, but the response can be lost before the client receives it. Retrying with no identity can apply the same logical operation twice. Avoiding the retry is not safe either, because the client cannot know whether the first request ran.\\n\\nEach transaction operation will therefore have its own stable operation ID. The client will create one ID for one logical request and reuse it when retrying that request. Before changing participant state, the operation will be registered with the coordinator. After it finishes, its confirmed effects will be merged into the working set.\\n\\nParticipants will keep recent results long enough to recognize a retry. If a response was lost, the same operation can return its previous result and complete its registration without applying the change again. Reusing an ID for a different request will be rejected.\\n\\nThere is a deliberate safety choice here. If the coordinator knows that an operation may be running but cannot confirm its result, commit must wait or ask the caller to retry. A temporarily stuck transaction is preferable to a successful commit that silently misses a write.\\n\\n## A Reusable Core, Not a Second Script Engine\\n\\nKahuna already has strong transaction machinery inside its scripting path. It includes read validation, two-phase commit, lock cleanup, and rollback behavior. However, the generic coordination logic and the script executor currently live too close together.\\n\\nWe want to separate these responsibilities. The coordinator will manage transaction identity, lifecycle, working sets, operation registration, finalization, and cleanup. The script executor will continue to manage parsing, variables, expressions, and script-specific execution.\\n\\nThe script engine can then use the coordinator rather than define what a transaction coordinator is. Interactive clients and other systems will use the same core guarantees without carrying script concepts into their own transaction flow.\\n\\nThis separation is also important for reuse beyond the .NET client. The protocol should describe stable behavior that any SDK can follow: keep the transaction handle, keep one operation ID per logical request, and let the server own coordination state.\\n\\n## Finalization Is a State Machine\\n\\nCommit and rollback are not simple buttons. Duplicate requests can arrive at the same time, a retry can overlap with an earlier attempt, and an abandoned transaction can be cleaned up while a client is still waiting for a response.\\n\\nThe coordinator will serialize these paths through one finalization state. If an attempt is already active, duplicate requests will wait for and observe the same result. Once the transaction reaches a terminal outcome, that outcome will not change. A committed transaction stays committed, and a rolled-back transaction stays rolled back.\\n\\nNot every unsuccessful attempt is terminal. A temporary routing problem, an unavailable participant, or an operation that has not finished may mean \u201cretry this same action.\u201d In that case, the finalization slot must become available for a later attempt. A retryable response should not permanently consume a one-shot gate.\\n\\nThe same rule will apply to automatic cleanup. The reaper that handles expired sessions must coordinate with explicit commit and rollback calls, rather than racing them from a separate path.\\n\\n## Concurrency Choices Should Be Explicit\\n\\nDifferent applications need different transaction behavior. Some want pessimistic locking. Others prefer optimistic concurrency and validate their reads at commit. Snapshot-based systems may already have their own read timestamp and should not repeat the same validation in Kahuna.\\n\\nThe transaction will begin with an explicit policy that covers locking, read behavior, timeout, release behavior, and decision durability. Invalid combinations will be rejected early instead of being interpreted in a surprising way.\\n\\nThis is especially helpful when Kahuna is used as a coordination layer for another database. The database can keep its own isolation rules while relying on Kahuna for the shared transaction lifecycle. The boundary between the two systems becomes visible and testable.\\n\\n## Durable Commit Decisions, with Honest Limits\\n\\nThe most demanding part of the work is recovering after a commit decision has been made but before every participant has acknowledged it.\\n\\nFor persistent data, we plan to offer an opt-in durable decision mode. Before sending the first commit command to a participant, the coordinator will replicate the commit decision. Each participant will record a durable completion receipt together with the committed value. The coordinator will then persist acknowledgement progress and continue retrying unfinished participants after a coordinator restart or leadership change.\\n\\nThe order matters. First, the participant commits the value and its receipt. Next, the coordinator records that acknowledgement durably. Only then may the participant forget the receipt. This prevents a delayed retry from confusing \u201calready committed\u201d with \u201cunknown transaction.\u201d\\n\\nDurable decisions will cost more coordination and replication, so they will not be the default for every workload. They will also reject transactions that modify ephemeral values. A recoverable commit decision cannot promise that in-memory data will survive a process loss.\\n\\nWe also want to be precise about what this mode does not solve. The active interactive session will still be held in memory before the commit decision. Prepared participant state will not become durable as part of this work. If a participant loses leadership after prepare but before its commit, the new leader may not have enough information to finish. The correct response in that case is to report that the operation must be retried, not to claim a guarantee the system cannot provide.\\n\\nGood distributed-system design is not only about adding stronger guarantees. It is also about making the remaining boundaries clear.\\n\\n## What This Means for Clients\\n\\nThe client-side transaction session will become lighter. It will retain the opaque handle, policy, operation IDs, and local status, but it will no longer be the authority for keys, locks, or read dependencies. Client-side synchronization may still improve the developer experience, while server-side coordination will provide the actual correctness.\\n\\nOther systems built on Kahuna will be able to remove duplicate coordination state. They may still keep their own session registry, isolation mode, cache protocol, schema information, and application timeouts. Those are domain responsibilities. The reusable coordinator should own only the shared mechanics that are easy to get wrong: operation fencing, working-set tracking, finalization, retries, and cleanup.\\n\\nThere is a performance trade-off. Registering and confirming each interactive operation adds coordinator traffic. We will measure point operations, batches, validated reads, and snapshot reads before deciding where batching, locality, or streaming can reduce the cost. The first goal is a correct protocol; the next is making that protocol efficient.\\n\\n## The Direction\\n\\nThe result we are aiming for is not a magical transaction system where failures disappear. It is a coordinator with understandable ownership and predictable answers.\\n\\nClients should be able to say, \u201cthis is my transaction,\u201d without also carrying a fragile summary of everything it has done. Duplicate requests should converge on one outcome. A late operation should never slip past commit. Durable mode should recover the decisions it truly knows, and best-effort mode should remain honest about what can be lost.\\n\\nThat foundation will make Kahuna transactions easier to use directly and easier to reuse inside larger systems. More importantly, it will move the hardest correctness rules into one place, where we can test them under concurrency, retries, leadership changes, and process failures.\\n\\nThat is the kind of simplicity we want: not fewer moving parts inside the system, but fewer ways for its users to make a distributed transaction unsafe."},{"id":"welcome","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/welcome","editUrl":"https://github.com/kahunakv.github.io/tree/tree/main/blog/2025-03-25-welcome/index.md","source":"@site/blog/2025-03-25-welcome/index.md","title":"Why I Built Kahuna - A New Take on Distributed Key/Value Storage","description":"About eight years ago, I had the opportunity to build systems for my company using Erlang. Discovering the actor model was a turning point in how I designed systems from that moment on. However, for strategic reasons, we had to move away from Erlang and focus on the .NET ecosystem. While robust and well-established actor model libraries for C# exist, they included many features we didn\u2019t need, and their performance wasn\u2019t entirely satisfactory for our use cases.","date":"2025-03-25T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"kahuna","permalink":"/blog/tags/kahuna"}],"readingTime":5.96,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Andres Gutierrez","title":"Creator of Kahuna","url":"https://github.com/andresgutierrez","imageURL":"https://github.com/andresgutierrez.png","key":"andresgutierrez","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"welcome","title":"Why I Built Kahuna - A New Take on Distributed Key/Value Storage","authors":["andresgutierrez"],"tags":["kahuna"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Making Distributed Transactions Easier to Trust","permalink":"/blog/reusable-transaction-coordinator"}},"content":"About eight years ago, I had the opportunity to build systems for my company using **[Erlang](https://www.erlang.org/)**. Discovering the actor model was a turning point in how I designed systems from that moment on. However, for strategic reasons, we had to move away from Erlang and focus on the .NET ecosystem. While robust and well-established actor model libraries for C# exist, they included many features we didn\u2019t need, and their performance wasn\u2019t entirely satisfactory for our use cases. \\nThis led me to the idea of building a lightweight library.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\nThe goal of creating a simple library led me to develop **[Nixie](https://github.com/andresgutierrez/nixie)**. By leveraging **[lock-free structures](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/standard/collections/thread-safe/)**, strongly typed actors, low-level programming, and the **[Task Processing Library (TPL)](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/standard/parallel-programming/task-parallel-library-tpl)**, I built a powerful foundation for application development. It immediately delivered the results we expected: fewer errors and higher performance.\\n\\nLater, the need arose to develop a leader election library for one of our critical systems, one that wouldn\u2019t rely on an external system, would offer fault tolerance (electing a new leader if the current one failed or went down), and could be integrated into our .NET projects. It was also essential to support multiple partitions (sometimes called regions, groups, or tablets) to effectively utilize computing power without sacrificing consistency. The learning experience and hands-on work with Raft led me to open-source Kommander.\\n\\n**[Kommander](https://github.com/andresgutierrez/kommander)** is a distributed consensus library for C#/.NET based on **[Raft](https://raft.github.io/)**. It supports multiple Raft groups, each with its own leader, followers, and replication log. It enables node communication via **[gRPC](https://grpc.io/)** (by default) or **REST/JSON** (for those who want or need to use any HTTP protocol 1.1/2/3 or require easy debugging). For the persistent write-ahead log (storage), it leverages the robust and battle-tested **[RocksDB](https://rocksdb.org/)** (created by Meta) as an embedded database, with **SQLite** also available as an option.\\n\\nKommander is built on Nixie, meaning processes like leader election, replication, compaction, and membership changes are handled using lightweight actors (state machines) and asynchronous communication. This approach enables hundreds or even thousands of partitions (groups), leveraging multi-core servers efficiently. Additionally, the actor model helps avoid multithreading concurrency issues, reducing the risk of hard-to-reproduce errors and lock contention.\\n\\nWith Kommander and Nixie providing a solid foundation, I recognized additional needs that could be addressed with a new tool based on this technology. This led to the idea of building an auxiliary tool to centralize distributed concerns such as locks, leader election, configuration management, sessions, caching, sequencing, and more.\\n\\nIn the past, we relied on various tools like Redis, Zookeeper, and even database tables to solve these challenges. However, it was easy to misuse a tool or assume it provided guarantees or performance levels that it actually didn\u2019t.\\n\\nTaking all of this into account, I created Kahuna.\\n\\n## Why the Name?\\n\\nThe name \\"Kahuna\\" comes from a Hawaiian word traditionally used to describe an expert in a field\u2014a fitting metaphor for a tool that aims to be the expert all services consult for coordination and operation. Inspired by battle-tested systems like Redis, etcd, Zookeeper, and Google Spanner, Kahuna is more than just another key/value store.\\n\\n## Why .NET? Breaking the Language Paradigm\\n\\nOne of my primary motivations for building Kahuna was to demonstrate that powerful, low-level systems infrastructure isn\u2019t limited to C++, Rust, Java, or Go. By implementing Kahuna in modern C# and .NET technologies, I wanted to challenge the prevailing notion that high-performance distributed systems can only be built with traditional systems programming languages. This isn\u2019t a hard rule\u2014there are great tools like Garnet, recently led by Microsoft. Similarly, in Java (a language with a garbage collector), you can find powerful distributed systems like Kafka, Cassandra, and Spark. This choice reflects not just a technical preference but a broader vision of expanding the distributed systems development ecosystem.\\n\\n## The Three Pillars of Kahuna\\n\\nKahuna brings together three critical distributed system primitives in one platform:  \\n\\n1. **A Distributed Key/Value Store** \u2013 for metadata storage, configuration, caching, sessions, and more.  \\n2. **A Distributed Locking System** \u2013 for coordinating access to shared resources in a cluster.  \\n3. **A Distributed Sequencer** \u2013 for generating globally ordered events, crucial for ensuring consistency in distributed workflows.  \\n\\nMany systems provide these functionalities separately, but I wanted **a single, unified solution** that integrates all three while maintaining strong consistency, high availability, simplicity, and efficient scalability.\\n\\n## Why Kahuna?  \\n\\n### **Distributed Transactions with Snapshot Isolation or Serializable Consistency**  \\nKahuna supports multi-node **distributed transactions** with **optimistic or pessimistic locking**, allowing developers to balance performance and strict consistency. Transactions can achieve either:  \\n- **Snapshot Isolation** (MVCC-based) for high-performance reads without conflicts.  \\n- **Serializable Consistency** for the strongest level of isolation.  \\n\\nThis makes Kahuna ideal for applications requiring strict data integrity guarantees in highly concurrent environments.\\n\\n### **Two Durability Modes: Persistent & Ephemeral**  \\nNot all data needs permanent storage. Kahuna supports **two durability modes** to optimize storage and performance:  \\n\\n- **Persistent Mode**: Data is replicated across nodes for **durability and fault tolerance**, ensuring high availability even during failures.  \\n- **Ephemeral Mode**: Designed for **short-lived objects** such as **caching, short sessions, and temporary leases/locks**, ensuring lightweight storage without unnecessary replication overhead.  \\n\\nThis flexibility allows developers to use Kahuna for both **long-term state storage and temporary coordination needs.**\\n\\n### **A More Powerful Approach to Distributed Locking**  \\nDistributed locking is **notoriously difficult** to get right, and perfect distributed locks are nearly impossible. Kahuna provides **tools to help developers mitigate edge cases**, including:  \\n\\n- **Persisted Lock State**: Ensuring another node can maintain the lock state if the leader fails.  \\n- **Leases with Expiration**: Preventing locks from persisting indefinitely if the holder crashes.  \\n- **Fencing Tokens**: Preventing stale lock holders from causing race conditions.  \\n\\nThese mechanisms **greatly reduce** the risk of deadlocks, split-brain scenarios, and other failure conditions common in distributed environments.\\n\\n## **Scripting Language Integration**  \\nA unique feature of Kahuna is its built-in scripting language, allowing developers to create consistent, transactional code that interacts directly with the key/value store.\\n\\n### **Strong Consistency with Raft-Based Consensus**  \\nKahuna ensures **strong consistency** using the **Raft consensus algorithm**, with each partition managed by its own **Raft group**. This design provides:  \\n\\n- **Fault tolerance**: Automatic leader election and replication ensure high availability.  \\n- **Consistency guarantees**: Every write is **strongly consistent** across multiple replicas.  \\n\\n### **Scalability Through Dynamic Partitioning**  \\nKahuna is **horizontally scalable**, meaning it expands with infrastructure growth. It supports:  \\n\\n- **Dynamic partitioning** (similar to sharding), allowing for **load balancing across nodes**.  \\n- **Automatic rebalancing**, ensuring even workload distribution.  \\n\\n## Kahuna: A Unified Solution for Modern Distributed Applications  \\n\\nKahuna provides a **powerful yet flexible** foundation for building **reliable, scalable, and highly available** distributed applications. By integrating **storage, coordination, and sequencing** into one system, it reduces complexity and offers developers a **consistent, battle-tested approach to managing distributed workloads.**\\n\\nKahuna is now **open-source**, and I\u2019d love to hear your thoughts! Whether you need a **transactional key/value store, a robust distributed lock system, or a global event sequencer**, **Kahuna is built to handle it all.**"}]}}')}}]);